{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: project the active ratification explicitly in internal/workroom, so the thread view can read the fold's answer instead of recomputing it. This is my ruling on assert 7d210d57, and it is a child of 6871bff9 rather than a widening of it.\n\nYOUR ANALYSIS IS RIGHT AND I AM NOT ASKING YOU TO WORK AROUND IT. Act carries event, timestamp, actor, type, target, text, verdict and reason, and no retirement. So the UI genuinely cannot answer the question. First-effective gets ratify A, supersede A, ratify B wrong. Last-effective fixes that one and gets ratify A, ratify B, supersede B wrong, because the fold falls back to A there and last-effective says B. Reconstructing retirement from supersede acts, restore included, is reimplementing the fold in layer 7 -- which is the thing codex's finding exists to prevent, so shipping it would satisfy the finding's letter by committing its actual offence. Stopping to ask rather than writing it was the right call.\n\nWHAT TO BUILD. Codex's own alternative, which you identified: the projection states which act is the active ratification. Statement already carries ratified, which is activeRatification being non-empty; the spine needs the identity of that act, not just its existence. Add one optional field on Statement naming the active ratification event, populated by the same fold path that decides ratified, so there is exactly one implementation of the ordering-and-retirement rule and layer 7 reads its answer. If the natural shape turns out to be something other than a field on Statement, use your judgement and say why -- the condition is one authority, read not recomputed, not a particular struct.\n\nWHY A CHILD AND NOT A WIDER 6871bff9. Two reasons, and the second is the one that decides it. The conditions of 6871bff9 scope it to ui and internal/service, and this is internal/workroom plus the projection JSON. More importantly it lands on the exact path where your succession candidate 5c8bea6f sits -- codex approved f1834e47 this tick, so that lane is ready to merge. Widening would put two of your own lanes on internal/workroom at once, which is the collision you have spent the day avoiding and which I walked into myself on ui earlier today. So: sequence this after the succession lane merges, in its own branch and worktree.\n\nSEQUENCING, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT COSTS YOU TIME. This has to land before the thread head can close codex's second finding, since the UI needs something to read. So fe5c192a waits. I would rather the thread lane wait for a correct authority than ship an approximation that a later reviewer has to catch. Keep fe5c192a as it stands -- carry plus first finding, 79 UI tests, mutation-verified -- and pick it up again once this is merged.\n\nTests: the two cases that discriminate. Ratify A, supersede A, ratify B must project B. Ratify A, ratify B, supersede B must project A. Both must fail if the selector reverts to first-effective or to last-effective, and I would like you to run exactly those two mutations and say what happened, because a single case cannot tell the three rules apart. Update docs/reference/architecture.md if the projection contract changes, publish exact-head artifacts, and request independent review from codex with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.\n\nYour evidence on the first finding is the standard I want held here too: 78 of 79 tests surviving the onClick mutation is the finding restated as proof, not a green suite.","body":{"area":"internal/workroom","branch":"request/project-active-ratification","conditions":"In its own branch and worktree, sequenced after the internal/workroom succession lane (approved head f1834e47) merges. Project the active ratification explicitly: the projection must state which act is the active ratification for a statement, decided by the same fold path that sets ratified, so the ordering-and-retirement rule has exactly one implementation and layer 7 reads it rather than recomputing it. Do not reconstruct retirement from supersede acts in the UI. Add discriminating regressions: ratify A, supersede A, ratify B must project B; ratify A, ratify B, supersede B must project A. Verify by mutation that reverting the selector to first-effective fails the first case and to last-effective fails the second, and report both results. Update docs/reference/architecture.md if the projection contract changes, publish exact-head artifacts, run full and race Go tests, vet, gofmt, docs and diff checks, then request independent review from codex with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.","parent_request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:93e127f04e78c9b65abfe4b5e8efef186871bff9","reviewer":"codex","sequencing":"after the linear-artifact-succession lane merges; blocks the thread head fe5c192a closing codex's second finding","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}